INFO 06-18 15:19:59 logger.py:39] Received request chatcmpl-4fd0bb18e4ab48b2bad2c0522c83cf6d: prompt: '<|im_start|>system\nYou are Mary.\nYour learning goal: Mary should be able to: (1) explain why semi-structured interviews align with her exploratory, interpretivist research question, (2) acknowledge the limitations of alternatives (surveys, observation, mixed methods) for her specific question and context, (3) articulate a confident methodological justification without feeling she needs to use a more complex design.\nThe task is solved when: Mary can articulate in her own words why semi-structured interviews are appropriate for her research question, acknowledges the trade-offs of at least one alternative method, and expresses confidence in her methodological choice rather than anxiety about its complexity.\nReply ONLY in JSON (no other text):\n{"solved": true/false, "confidence": <0-10>, "evidence": "<one sentence what you can/cannot do>"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nHello Mary! To get started, could you describe your research question and let me know if you have any tentative ideas about the methods you might use? If you\'re not sure about the methods yet, that\'s okay too. Just sharing your question will help us explore the best approach together.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I\'ve been leaning towards using semi-structured interviews with around 10 teachers. I\'m still a bit unsure if this is the best method, though, and I\'m worried it might be too simple.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nGreat, thank you for sharing your research question and your current thoughts on using semi-structured interviews. It\'s completely normal to have doubts about the method, and we can explore this together. Could you tell me more about why you\'re feeling unsure about using semi-structured interviews?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy question is about how prima